1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:04,156
If you asked most Americans
to point to the center of Amish life,

2
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:06,396
they would probably choose Pennsylvania,

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:08,736
and the numbers seem to prove them right.

4
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:10,296
In twenty twenty-five,

5
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:14,636
Pennsylvania had an estimated ninety-
five thousand four hundred and ten Amish

6
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:15,656
residents.

7
00:00:15,740 --> 00:00:19,656
Wisconsin had only twenty-seven
thousand five hundred thirty-five.

8
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:23,540
So Pennsylvania has well over
three times as many Amish people.

9
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:26,080
But now look at the map
in a different way.

10
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:29,096
Pennsylvania has sixty-
three Amish settlements.

11
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:31,140
Wisconsin has sixty-eight.

12
00:00:31,240 --> 00:00:32,296
Five more.

13
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:35,396
Wisconsin actually has more
separate Amish communities than

14
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:39,940
the state most closely associated with
the Amish in the American imagination.

15
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:42,376
And that is not even the strangest part.

16
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:45,876
One Amish settlement around
Lancaster County, Pennsylvania,

17
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:50,076
contains roughly forty-four thousand
seven hundred sixty-five people.

18
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:54,636
That single settlement is larger than
the entire Amish population of Wisconsin.

19
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:57,836
Yet Wisconsin still has
more settlements statewide.

20
00:00:57,920 --> 00:00:59,960
So what exactly are we counting?

21
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:02,616
And why would twenty-
seven thousand Amish people

22
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:05,336
spread themselves across
more separate communities

23
00:01:05,420 --> 00:01:08,280
than ninety-five thousand
people in Pennsylvania?

24
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:13,456
The answer is not that Wisconsin somehow
became more Amish than Pennsylvania.

25
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:15,456
It is that these two states represent

26
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:19,336
two completely different ways
an Amish population can grow.

27
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:21,376
Pennsylvania built giants.

28
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:23,236
Wisconsin built dots.

29
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:24,956
And once you understand why,

30
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,636
the entire map of Amish America
starts to look different.

31
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:30,336
The first thing to understand

32
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:33,916
is that an Amish settlement is
not the same thing as a town.

33
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,216
It does not necessarily have a mayor.

34
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:37,856
It may not have a sign.

35
00:01:37,940 --> 00:01:39,900
It may cross county lines.

36
00:01:40,020 --> 00:01:43,956
And sometimes an English-speaking
neighbor living five miles away

37
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:48,476
might not even realize that researchers
consider the Amish families around him

38
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:50,336
part of a distinct settlement.

39
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:54,336
Researchers at the Young Center
for Anabaptist and Pietist Studies

40
00:01:54,420 --> 00:01:55,676
define a settlement

41
00:01:55,760 --> 00:02:00,496
as a geographic Amish community
containing one or more church districts.

42
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:03,836
And the church district is
the more important building block.

43
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:05,476
An Amish church district

44
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:08,656
generally contains around
twenty to forty households.

45
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:10,976
There is normally no giant church building

46
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:14,900
where hundreds or thousands of Amish
gather together every Sunday.

47
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:17,356
Worship usually takes place in homes,

48
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:19,720
barns, or other local spaces.

49
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:23,476
That limits how large
a congregation can comfortably become.

50
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:26,236
So when enough families
accumulate in one area,

51
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:28,300
the church district divides.

52
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:29,836
One becomes two,

53
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:31,362
two become three,

54
00:02:31,446 --> 00:02:32,986
three become ten.

55
00:02:33,206 --> 00:02:37,342
But all ten can still belong to the same
settlement if they remain geographically

56
00:02:37,426 --> 00:02:38,426
connected.

57
00:02:38,626 --> 00:02:41,302
That distinction explains
almost everything.

58
00:02:41,386 --> 00:02:42,642
A settlement tells you

59
00:02:42,726 --> 00:02:46,162
how many geographically
separate Amish communities exist.

60
00:02:46,246 --> 00:02:48,562
A church district tells
you much more about

61
00:02:48,646 --> 00:02:51,502
how large those communities
have become internally.

62
00:02:51,586 --> 00:02:55,562
And suddenly, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin
stop looking like a contradiction.

63
00:02:55,646 --> 00:02:58,862
Pennsylvania has six hundred
and thirty-six church districts.

64
00:02:58,946 --> 00:03:01,406
Wisconsin has only two hundred and nine.

65
00:03:01,726 --> 00:03:04,942
So Wisconsin wins
on the number of separate dots.

66
00:03:05,026 --> 00:03:07,402
Pennsylvania absolutely dominates in how

67
00:03:07,486 --> 00:03:11,042
much of the Amish population
is packed inside those dots.

68
00:03:11,126 --> 00:03:13,162
The best example is Lancaster.

69
00:03:13,246 --> 00:03:15,922
The Lancaster County area Amish settlement

70
00:03:16,006 --> 00:03:20,606
is one of the oldest continuously existing
Amish communities in North America.

71
00:03:20,706 --> 00:03:22,162
In twenty twenty-five,

72
00:03:22,246 --> 00:03:24,341
researchers estimated it at roughly

73
00:03:24,425 --> 00:03:27,402
forty-four thousand seven
hundred and sixty-five people

74
00:03:27,486 --> 00:03:30,182
and two hundred and sixty-
seven church districts.

75
00:03:30,266 --> 00:03:31,902
Pause on that second number.

76
00:03:31,986 --> 00:03:36,186
The entire state of Wisconsin has
only two hundred and nine districts,

77
00:03:36,286 --> 00:03:39,662
meaning Lancaster's single
giant geographic settlement

78
00:03:39,746 --> 00:03:42,762
contains more individual
Amish congregations

79
00:03:42,846 --> 00:03:46,226
than every Amish settlement
in Wisconsin combined.

80
00:03:46,346 --> 00:03:49,622
One Pennsylvania dot on the map
contains more church districts

81
00:03:49,706 --> 00:03:51,882
than sixty-eight Wisconsin dots.

82
00:03:51,966 --> 00:03:55,986
And Lancaster is not Pennsylvania's
only large community.

83
00:03:56,166 --> 00:03:57,522
Quick question, would

84
00:03:57,606 --> 00:04:02,186
you want to see more videos like this from
communities most people barely understand?

85
00:04:02,326 --> 00:04:06,026
If yes, subscribe because
there's a lot more coming.

86
00:04:06,166 --> 00:04:09,602
The Mifflin County area has
around thirty-six districts

87
00:04:09,686 --> 00:04:13,086
and more than five thousand
five hundred Amish residents.

88
00:04:13,186 --> 00:04:17,026
The Indiana County area has
around twenty-five districts.

89
00:04:17,246 --> 00:04:21,942
Pennsylvania has several old communities
where generation after generation

90
00:04:22,026 --> 00:04:25,526
has accumulated in roughly
the same geographic zone.

91
00:04:25,726 --> 00:04:28,622
This is what a mature
Amish settlement looks like.

92
00:04:28,706 --> 00:04:31,362
It does not necessarily
create a new settlement

93
00:04:31,446 --> 00:04:33,562
every time the population grows.

94
00:04:33,646 --> 00:04:36,622
Often, it simply keeps
dividing internally.

95
00:04:36,706 --> 00:04:38,242
A new district forms,

96
00:04:38,326 --> 00:04:39,282
then another.

97
00:04:39,366 --> 00:04:41,602
The map still shows one settlement,

98
00:04:41,686 --> 00:04:43,502
but underneath that single dot,

99
00:04:43,586 --> 00:04:45,642
the population is multiplying.

100
00:04:45,726 --> 00:04:48,822
This is why simply counting
settlements can fool you.

101
00:04:48,906 --> 00:04:50,642
If you looked only at dots,

102
00:04:50,726 --> 00:04:52,502
Lancaster counts as one.

103
00:04:52,586 --> 00:04:57,762
A brand-new Amish community with 15 people
somewhere else can also count as one.

104
00:04:57,846 --> 00:05:00,103
On a map, both receive a dot.

105
00:05:00,187 --> 00:05:01,103
In reality,

106
00:05:01,187 --> 00:05:03,907
they are completely
different demographic worlds.

107
00:05:04,007 --> 00:05:07,367
Wisconsin has some substantial
Amish communities too.

108
00:05:07,487 --> 00:05:10,523
Cashton has grown
into the state's largest settlement.

109
00:05:10,607 --> 00:05:12,743
Kingston is another major center.

110
00:05:12,827 --> 00:05:14,503
Augusta, Hillsboro,

111
00:05:14,587 --> 00:05:15,523
Wilton, Tomah,

112
00:05:15,607 --> 00:05:18,163
and several others have
existed for decades.

113
00:05:18,247 --> 00:05:21,987
But most of Wisconsin's map
is not one enormous community.

114
00:05:22,087 --> 00:05:24,083
It is a network of smaller ones.

115
00:05:24,167 --> 00:05:25,703
In 2025,

116
00:05:25,787 --> 00:05:30,083
Wisconsin's twenty-seven thousand five
hundred thirty-five Amish residents

117
00:05:30,167 --> 00:05:32,607
were spread across 68 settlements.

118
00:05:32,827 --> 00:05:36,623
That works out to roughly four
hundred and five people per settlement

119
00:05:36,707 --> 00:05:39,043
if you use a simple statewide average.

120
00:05:39,127 --> 00:05:42,403
Pennsylvania averages more than
one thousand five hundred people

121
00:05:42,487 --> 00:05:43,563
per settlement.

122
00:05:43,647 --> 00:05:46,523
The church district
comparison is even clearer.

123
00:05:46,607 --> 00:05:50,203
Pennsylvania averages roughly
10 districts per settlement.

124
00:05:50,287 --> 00:05:52,983
Wisconsin averages only about three,

125
00:05:53,067 --> 00:05:57,643
and many Wisconsin communities are far
smaller than that average suggests.

126
00:05:57,727 --> 00:05:59,023
In Adams County,

127
00:05:59,107 --> 00:06:01,783
a settlement established in 2023

128
00:06:01,867 --> 00:06:04,707
was estimated at only about 40 people.

129
00:06:04,867 --> 00:06:05,863
Camp Douglas,

130
00:06:05,947 --> 00:06:07,743
founded in 2024,

131
00:06:07,827 --> 00:06:10,167
was estimated at roughly 15.

132
00:06:10,307 --> 00:06:13,143
Warrens, established in 2023,

133
00:06:13,227 --> 00:06:14,487
around 20.

134
00:06:14,667 --> 00:06:17,943
Other settlements consist of one
district and perhaps a few dozen

135
00:06:18,027 --> 00:06:19,647
or a few hundred people.

136
00:06:19,787 --> 00:06:20,923
At the same time,

137
00:06:21,007 --> 00:06:24,667
older Wisconsin communities
like Cashton contain thousands.

138
00:06:24,827 --> 00:06:27,963
So Wisconsin is not one
unified Amish belt.

139
00:06:28,047 --> 00:06:30,007
It is more like an archipelago.

140
00:06:30,167 --> 00:06:31,403
Large communities,

141
00:06:31,487 --> 00:06:32,883
medium communities,

142
00:06:32,967 --> 00:06:34,407
tiny communities.

143
00:06:34,627 --> 00:06:36,707
Some are only a few years old,

144
00:06:36,907 --> 00:06:40,943
all separated enough geographically
to count as different settlements.

145
00:06:41,027 --> 00:06:43,027
And that raises the real question.

146
00:06:43,247 --> 00:06:45,547
Why did Wisconsin develop this way?

147
00:06:45,687 --> 00:06:47,623
The answer starts with movement.

148
00:06:47,707 --> 00:06:50,527
Wisconsin's Amish map was built in waves.

149
00:06:50,667 --> 00:06:53,523
Some early settlements appeared
more than a century ago,

150
00:06:53,607 --> 00:06:54,963
but several failed.

151
00:06:55,047 --> 00:06:56,563
Families moved away.

152
00:06:56,647 --> 00:06:57,903
Ministers left.

153
00:06:57,987 --> 00:06:59,623
Communities disappeared.

154
00:06:59,707 --> 00:07:01,483
Then new groups arrived later

155
00:07:01,567 --> 00:07:03,943
and started again in different places.

156
00:07:04,027 --> 00:07:06,363
The modern Wisconsin Amish landscape

157
00:07:06,447 --> 00:07:10,487
really accelerated during
the second half of the 20th century.

158
00:07:10,627 --> 00:07:13,343
Cashton dates to the 1960s.

159
00:07:13,427 --> 00:07:16,523
Wilton-Tomah also emerged
around that period.

160
00:07:16,607 --> 00:07:19,243
Kingston took shape in the 1970s.

161
00:07:19,327 --> 00:07:20,703
Augusta followed.

162
00:07:20,787 --> 00:07:24,223
Then came communities
established during the 1980s,

163
00:07:24,307 --> 00:07:25,678
1990s,

164
00:07:25,762 --> 00:07:26,858
2000s,

165
00:07:26,942 --> 00:07:27,938
2010s,

166
00:07:28,022 --> 00:07:29,858
and even the 2020s.

167
00:07:29,942 --> 00:07:30,958
In other words,

168
00:07:31,042 --> 00:07:35,898
Wisconsin did not receive one giant
migration that settled in one valley

169
00:07:35,982 --> 00:07:37,262
and stayed there.

170
00:07:37,442 --> 00:07:40,278
Different Amish groups
arrived at different times,

171
00:07:40,362 --> 00:07:43,942
often from different states
and different religious affiliations.

172
00:07:44,122 --> 00:07:49,098
That matters because the Amish are
not one centrally controlled organization.

173
00:07:49,182 --> 00:07:52,138
There is no national
Amish headquarters deciding

174
00:07:52,222 --> 00:07:54,878
this county gets five hundred families.

175
00:07:54,962 --> 00:07:58,798
Individual church communities differ
in how they regulate technology,

176
00:07:58,882 --> 00:07:59,958
occupations,

177
00:08:00,042 --> 00:08:03,142
farming practices,
and other details of daily life.

178
00:08:03,402 --> 00:08:06,118
Families often prefer
to settle near other Amish

179
00:08:06,202 --> 00:08:09,082
whose church practices are
compatible with their own.

180
00:08:09,282 --> 00:08:11,718
So when Amish migration enters a state,

181
00:08:11,802 --> 00:08:15,038
it does not necessarily merge
into one existing community.

182
00:08:15,122 --> 00:08:17,658
Sometimes the better
fit is somewhere else,

183
00:08:17,742 --> 00:08:19,058
a different county,

184
00:08:19,142 --> 00:08:20,418
a different cluster,

185
00:08:20,502 --> 00:08:21,878
a different settlement,

186
00:08:21,962 --> 00:08:22,818
and Wisconsin has

187
00:08:22,902 --> 00:08:26,358
become a place where many of those
separate streams have landed.

188
00:08:26,442 --> 00:08:29,418
There is a revealing example from 1990.

189
00:08:29,502 --> 00:08:33,118
A group of Amish men from the Spring
Run area of Pennsylvania

190
00:08:33,202 --> 00:08:35,642
traveled west to inspect Wisconsin.

191
00:08:35,742 --> 00:08:39,818
According to a recent academic
study of Wisconsin Amish migration,

192
00:08:39,902 --> 00:08:42,602
they were looking for two
very practical things,

193
00:08:42,782 --> 00:08:46,362
less expensive farms
and a workable milk market.

194
00:08:46,542 --> 00:08:49,442
They liked the area
around Athens, Wisconsin.

195
00:08:49,542 --> 00:08:51,322
A new settlement followed.

196
00:08:51,442 --> 00:08:53,158
That story is small,

197
00:08:53,242 --> 00:08:55,382
but the mechanism is enormous.

198
00:08:55,562 --> 00:08:58,678
For a young Amish family
trying to establish a farm,

199
00:08:58,762 --> 00:09:00,858
land is not simply an investment.

200
00:09:00,942 --> 00:09:04,878
It can determine whether the lifestyle
is economically possible at all.

201
00:09:04,962 --> 00:09:07,518
When an old settlement
grows for generations,

202
00:09:07,602 --> 00:09:10,178
farmland gets divided, purchased,

203
00:09:10,262 --> 00:09:12,878
inherited, and surrounded by development.

204
00:09:12,962 --> 00:09:15,542
More sons and daughters reach adulthood.

205
00:09:15,822 --> 00:09:17,978
More households need somewhere to live,

206
00:09:18,062 --> 00:09:21,217
but the amount of land does
not multiply with the population.

207
00:09:21,301 --> 00:09:22,158
Eventually,

208
00:09:22,242 --> 00:09:23,958
some families look outward,

209
00:09:24,042 --> 00:09:26,238
not because the old community is dying,

210
00:09:26,322 --> 00:09:28,338
often because it is doing the opposite.

211
00:09:28,422 --> 00:09:32,618
It has produced more families than
the local land market can easily absorb.

212
00:09:32,702 --> 00:09:34,718
And Wisconsin has repeatedly offered

213
00:09:34,802 --> 00:09:38,082
something older Amish regions
could not offer as easily,

214
00:09:38,202 --> 00:09:42,778
rural agricultural areas where a new
group could establish itself separately.

215
00:09:42,862 --> 00:09:46,162
But cheap land is only
one part of the story.

216
00:09:46,302 --> 00:09:47,258
The Young Center

217
00:09:47,342 --> 00:09:51,342
lists several reasons Amish
communities establish new settlements.

218
00:09:51,502 --> 00:09:52,938
Affordable farmland,

219
00:09:53,022 --> 00:09:55,238
access to suitable non-farm work,

220
00:09:55,322 --> 00:09:56,778
rural isolation,

221
00:09:56,862 --> 00:10:00,949
a local social and physical environment
compatible with their way of life,

222
00:10:01,069 --> 00:10:04,005
proximity to relatives
or similar church groups,

223
00:10:04,089 --> 00:10:08,265
and sometimes the need to resolve internal
church or leadership disagreements.

224
00:10:08,349 --> 00:10:09,425
That means two families

225
00:10:09,509 --> 00:10:13,025
can leave the same old settlement
for completely different reasons,

226
00:10:13,109 --> 00:10:17,769
and two groups arriving in Wisconsin may
intentionally settle in different places.

227
00:10:17,909 --> 00:10:21,069
More migration does
not always make one dot bigger.

228
00:10:21,209 --> 00:10:23,304
Sometimes it creates another dot.

229
00:10:23,388 --> 00:10:25,429
The movement is still happening.

230
00:10:25,529 --> 00:10:29,505
A twenty twenty-five study
examining household relocations

231
00:10:29,589 --> 00:10:31,645
into Wisconsin Amish settlements

232
00:10:31,729 --> 00:10:36,189
found that newcomers were not simply
shifting around inside the state.

233
00:10:36,429 --> 00:10:40,325
Among households in the study
that reported a prior location,

234
00:10:40,409 --> 00:10:45,309
a majority of those moving into Wisconsin
settlements had come from another state.

235
00:10:45,429 --> 00:10:49,625
A large share also moved from one
Wisconsin settlement to another,

236
00:10:49,709 --> 00:10:51,585
and the patterns were not random.

237
00:10:51,669 --> 00:10:53,105
Religious affiliation

238
00:10:53,189 --> 00:10:58,029
appeared to be an important factor in
deciding which settlement families chose.

239
00:10:58,149 --> 00:10:59,405
That is critical.

240
00:10:59,489 --> 00:11:02,465
Imagine Wisconsin not as one destination,

241
00:11:02,549 --> 00:11:05,309
but as dozens of potential destinations.

242
00:11:05,449 --> 00:11:07,305
A family does not simply ask,

243
00:11:07,389 --> 00:11:09,165
"Should we move to Wisconsin?"

244
00:11:09,249 --> 00:11:10,165
They may ask,

245
00:11:10,249 --> 00:11:12,485
"Which community has people we know?

246
00:11:12,569 --> 00:11:14,909
Which church practices match ours?

247
00:11:15,029 --> 00:11:16,725
Where is land available?

248
00:11:16,809 --> 00:11:18,325
Where can I make a living?

249
00:11:18,409 --> 00:11:19,785
Where are my relatives?

250
00:11:19,869 --> 00:11:24,189
Where can my children eventually find
spouses within a compatible community?"

251
00:11:24,349 --> 00:11:28,145
Those decisions create
a decentralized migration network.

252
00:11:28,229 --> 00:11:29,945
One family goes first.

253
00:11:30,029 --> 00:11:31,245
Relatives visit.

254
00:11:31,329 --> 00:11:33,305
Another property becomes available.

255
00:11:33,389 --> 00:11:35,205
A second household moves,

256
00:11:35,289 --> 00:11:36,345
then a third.

257
00:11:36,429 --> 00:11:39,845
Eventually, there are enough
families to support regular worship.

258
00:11:39,929 --> 00:11:41,325
A school may appear.

259
00:11:41,409 --> 00:11:42,809
Businesses follow.

260
00:11:42,929 --> 00:11:45,885
A tiny cluster becomes
a functioning settlement,

261
00:11:45,969 --> 00:11:47,865
and the map gets another dot.

262
00:11:47,949 --> 00:11:51,865
If you're enjoying stories about how
communities quietly reshape the map,

263
00:11:51,949 --> 00:11:52,925
subscribe,

264
00:11:53,009 --> 00:11:56,825
because this pattern goes much further
than Pennsylvania and Wisconsin.

265
00:11:56,909 --> 00:12:00,925
Now compare what happened from twenty
fifteen to twenty twenty-five.

266
00:12:01,009 --> 00:12:04,585
Pennsylvania went from fifty-
four settlements to sixty-three,

267
00:12:04,669 --> 00:12:06,145
a net gain of nine.

268
00:12:06,229 --> 00:12:07,965
But its church districts exploded

269
00:12:08,049 --> 00:12:11,285
from four hundred and sixty-five
to six hundred and thirty-six,

270
00:12:11,369 --> 00:12:14,265
an increase of one hundred
and seventy-one districts.

271
00:12:14,349 --> 00:12:16,485
That is classic internal growth.

272
00:12:16,569 --> 00:12:19,065
Existing communities
are getting much bigger.

273
00:12:19,149 --> 00:12:21,245
Wisconsin followed a different path.

274
00:12:21,329 --> 00:12:23,989
It went from fifty
settlements to sixty-eight,

275
00:12:24,189 --> 00:12:25,605
a gain of eighteen,

276
00:12:25,689 --> 00:12:28,585
twice Pennsylvania's
net settlement increase.

277
00:12:28,669 --> 00:12:30,925
Its church districts also grew

278
00:12:31,009 --> 00:12:34,071
from one hundred and thirty-
eight to two hundred and nine.

279
00:12:34,155 --> 00:12:36,791
but the geographic spread
is the striking part.

280
00:12:36,875 --> 00:12:39,491
Wisconsin kept adding separate communities

281
00:12:39,575 --> 00:12:43,731
while Pennsylvania's existing
giants continued dividing internally.

282
00:12:43,815 --> 00:12:44,891
Put another way,

283
00:12:44,975 --> 00:12:46,471
over that decade, Pennsylvania

284
00:12:46,555 --> 00:12:50,931
added roughly one new settlement for every
nineteen additional church districts.

285
00:12:51,015 --> 00:12:55,391
Wisconsin added one new settlement for
roughly every four additional districts.

286
00:12:55,475 --> 00:12:58,451
Those are not exact rules
for how communities form,

287
00:12:58,535 --> 00:13:01,255
but the contrast reveals
the shape of growth.

288
00:13:01,395 --> 00:13:04,711
Pennsylvania's growth is heavily
concentrated inside established

289
00:13:04,795 --> 00:13:05,851
communities.

290
00:13:05,935 --> 00:13:08,971
Wisconsin is much more
geographically distributed,

291
00:13:09,055 --> 00:13:12,191
and Wisconsin is not even
the only state doing this.

292
00:13:12,275 --> 00:13:15,691
Missouri had sixty-four
settlements in twenty twenty-five.

293
00:13:15,775 --> 00:13:17,331
New York had sixty.

294
00:13:17,415 --> 00:13:19,331
Kentucky had fifty-five.

295
00:13:19,415 --> 00:13:21,191
Michigan had fifty-three.

296
00:13:21,275 --> 00:13:23,811
Ohio led them all with seventy-four.

297
00:13:23,895 --> 00:13:28,710
The old mental picture of Amish America
as Pennsylvania, Ohio, and Indiana

298
00:13:28,794 --> 00:13:31,191
is becoming less useful every year.

299
00:13:31,275 --> 00:13:34,055
Those states still
contain most of the people,

300
00:13:34,195 --> 00:13:36,855
but the settlement frontier is much wider.

301
00:13:36,975 --> 00:13:39,215
There is one important caution here.

302
00:13:39,355 --> 00:13:41,915
A settlement is a research category.

303
00:13:42,055 --> 00:13:45,411
It is not a permanent legal
boundary carved into stone.

304
00:13:45,495 --> 00:13:50,795
Researchers sometimes reconsider where
one settlement ends and another begins.

305
00:13:51,035 --> 00:13:53,391
That happened recently around Lancaster.

306
00:13:53,475 --> 00:13:54,331
For years,

307
00:13:54,415 --> 00:13:56,951
church districts
in southeastern York County

308
00:13:57,035 --> 00:14:00,095
were counted as part
of the Lancaster area settlement.

309
00:14:00,215 --> 00:14:03,631
As the York County community
developed more of its own identity,

310
00:14:03,715 --> 00:14:05,991
researchers began listing it separately.

311
00:14:06,075 --> 00:14:08,131
Nothing dramatic happened overnight.

312
00:14:08,215 --> 00:14:10,711
Thousands of people did not suddenly move.

313
00:14:10,795 --> 00:14:14,091
The classification changed
because the community itself

314
00:14:14,175 --> 00:14:17,515
had changed enough to be treated
as geographically distinct.

315
00:14:17,955 --> 00:14:19,991
That tells us something important.

316
00:14:20,075 --> 00:14:23,511
The exact difference between
sixty-eight Wisconsin settlements

317
00:14:23,595 --> 00:14:28,455
and sixty-three Pennsylvania settlements
should not be treated like a sports score.

318
00:14:28,575 --> 00:14:30,975
Five is not a magical number.

319
00:14:31,195 --> 00:14:33,211
Next year's totals may change.

320
00:14:33,295 --> 00:14:34,870
Communities can begin.

321
00:14:34,954 --> 00:14:36,071
They can fail.

322
00:14:36,155 --> 00:14:37,431
They can divide.

323
00:14:37,515 --> 00:14:39,671
Researchers can refine boundaries.

324
00:14:39,755 --> 00:14:42,111
The meaningful fact is not simply

325
00:14:42,195 --> 00:14:43,831
Wisconsin sixty-eight,

326
00:14:43,915 --> 00:14:45,895
Pennsylvania sixty-three.

327
00:14:46,095 --> 00:14:47,331
The meaningful fact

328
00:14:47,415 --> 00:14:49,915
is the structure underneath those numbers.

329
00:14:50,035 --> 00:14:54,651
One state contains many more people
concentrated in giant old settlements.

330
00:14:54,811 --> 00:14:57,707
The other spreads
a much smaller population

331
00:14:57,791 --> 00:15:01,111
across a remarkable number
of independent communities.

332
00:15:01,271 --> 00:15:02,707
That pattern is real,

333
00:15:02,791 --> 00:15:05,391
even if an individual boundary changes.

334
00:15:05,511 --> 00:15:07,791
And there is another reason this matters.

335
00:15:07,991 --> 00:15:11,587
The Amish population of North
America is not stable.

336
00:15:11,671 --> 00:15:13,311
It is growing rapidly.

337
00:15:13,431 --> 00:15:14,587
In two thousand,

338
00:15:14,671 --> 00:15:18,027
researchers estimated about
hundred and seventy-seven thousand

339
00:15:18,111 --> 00:15:20,271
nine hundred and ten Amish people.

340
00:15:20,511 --> 00:15:22,027
By twenty twenty-five,

341
00:15:22,111 --> 00:15:25,407
the estimate had reached roughly
four hundred and ten thousand

342
00:15:25,491 --> 00:15:27,391
nine hundred and fifty-five,

343
00:15:27,511 --> 00:15:30,891
an increase of about hundred
and thirty-one percent.

344
00:15:31,051 --> 00:15:34,067
The population roughly
doubles every twenty years.

345
00:15:34,151 --> 00:15:37,087
The main reasons are
unusually large families

346
00:15:37,171 --> 00:15:41,331
and a high percentage of young people
who eventually join the church as adults.

347
00:15:41,511 --> 00:15:44,146
That creates a simple geographic problem.

348
00:15:44,230 --> 00:15:48,111
Every generation needs more households
than the generation before it.

349
00:15:48,211 --> 00:15:51,207
Some communities absorb
those households internally.

350
00:15:51,291 --> 00:15:52,787
That is Pennsylvania.

351
00:15:52,871 --> 00:15:54,007
More homes,

352
00:15:54,091 --> 00:15:55,227
more businesses,

353
00:15:55,311 --> 00:15:56,971
more church districts.

354
00:15:57,071 --> 00:15:59,767
Existing settlement
gets denser and larger.

355
00:15:59,851 --> 00:16:03,491
But there is a limit to how much
expansion can happen in one place.

356
00:16:03,611 --> 00:16:05,871
Land becomes difficult to obtain.

357
00:16:05,971 --> 00:16:07,227
Jobs change.

358
00:16:07,311 --> 00:16:09,027
Development closes in.

359
00:16:09,111 --> 00:16:12,247
Or families simply prefer
another environment.

360
00:16:12,331 --> 00:16:14,487
Then the pressure escapes outward.

361
00:16:14,571 --> 00:16:16,267
A few households leave,

362
00:16:16,351 --> 00:16:18,707
and if enough compatible families follow,

363
00:16:18,791 --> 00:16:20,967
the community does not disappear.

364
00:16:21,051 --> 00:16:23,211
It reproduces somewhere else.

365
00:16:23,351 --> 00:16:26,807
That is why the Amish map expands
differently from the map of many

366
00:16:26,891 --> 00:16:28,811
other growing populations.

367
00:16:28,911 --> 00:16:31,691
Growth does not have
to produce a giant city.

368
00:16:31,791 --> 00:16:33,787
It can create another settlement,

369
00:16:33,871 --> 00:16:34,887
then another,

370
00:16:34,971 --> 00:16:36,011
then another.

371
00:16:36,111 --> 00:16:37,947
In twenty twenty-five alone,

372
00:16:38,031 --> 00:16:41,747
researchers recorded nine newly
established Amish settlements

373
00:16:41,831 --> 00:16:43,367
across North America,

374
00:16:43,451 --> 00:16:47,427
while no existing settlement
dissolved during that one-year period.

375
00:16:47,511 --> 00:16:49,826
And more than half
of all Amish settlements

376
00:16:49,910 --> 00:16:52,551
contain only a single church district.

377
00:16:52,691 --> 00:16:55,907
That means most dots
on the map are not Lancaster.

378
00:16:55,991 --> 00:16:57,127
They are small,

379
00:16:57,211 --> 00:16:58,427
some fragile,

380
00:16:58,511 --> 00:16:59,571
some new.

381
00:16:59,671 --> 00:17:01,207
Some may not survive,

382
00:17:01,291 --> 00:17:02,207
but a few of them

383
00:17:02,291 --> 00:17:04,667
could eventually become the next Cashton,

384
00:17:04,751 --> 00:17:06,171
the next Kingston,

385
00:17:06,331 --> 00:17:08,507
or generations from now,

386
00:17:08,591 --> 00:17:10,231
something much larger.

387
00:17:10,351 --> 00:17:12,547
Now go back to the original numbers.

388
00:17:12,631 --> 00:17:13,727
Pennsylvania,

389
00:17:13,811 --> 00:17:17,291
about ninety-five thousand four
hundred and ten Amish people,

390
00:17:17,411 --> 00:17:19,091
sixty-three settlements,

391
00:17:19,191 --> 00:17:21,831
six hundred and thirty-
six church districts.

392
00:17:21,951 --> 00:17:22,947
Wisconsin,

393
00:17:23,031 --> 00:17:26,507
about twenty-seven thousand five
hundred and thirty-five people,

394
00:17:26,627 --> 00:17:28,203
sixty-eight settlements,

395
00:17:28,287 --> 00:17:30,143
two hundred and nine districts.

396
00:17:30,227 --> 00:17:33,583
Pennsylvania has more than
three times the Amish population,

397
00:17:33,667 --> 00:17:36,487
almost three times
the number of church districts.

398
00:17:36,627 --> 00:17:39,183
Yet Wisconsin has five more settlements.

399
00:17:39,267 --> 00:17:41,783
The numbers no longer
contradict each other.

400
00:17:41,867 --> 00:17:45,347
They describe two different
stages of community development.

401
00:17:45,447 --> 00:17:47,603
Pennsylvania is filled with older,

402
00:17:47,687 --> 00:17:48,883
deeper roots.

403
00:17:48,967 --> 00:17:51,683
Wisconsin contains more separate shoots.

404
00:17:51,767 --> 00:17:56,763
Pennsylvania's average settlement holds
roughly one thousand five hundred people.

405
00:17:56,847 --> 00:17:58,863
Wisconsin's roughly four hundred.

406
00:17:58,947 --> 00:18:00,863
Pennsylvania's typical map dot,

407
00:18:00,947 --> 00:18:02,343
taken statewide,

408
00:18:02,427 --> 00:18:05,043
represents a much larger concentration.

409
00:18:05,127 --> 00:18:09,383
Wisconsin needs many more dots
to represent far fewer people,

410
00:18:09,467 --> 00:18:12,263
and that is why the map
feels wrong at first.

411
00:18:12,347 --> 00:18:16,883
We unconsciously assume one settlement
means one similar-sized community.

412
00:18:16,967 --> 00:18:17,987
It doesn't.

413
00:18:18,247 --> 00:18:22,067
A settlement can mean fifteen
people in a new rural cluster

414
00:18:22,267 --> 00:18:25,627
or nearly forty-five thousand
people around Lancaster.

415
00:18:25,787 --> 00:18:27,407
Both are counted once.

416
00:18:27,507 --> 00:18:31,463
So why does Wisconsin have more
Amish settlements than Pennsylvania?

417
00:18:31,547 --> 00:18:33,947
Not because Wisconsin has more Amish.

418
00:18:34,067 --> 00:18:35,063
It doesn't.

419
00:18:35,147 --> 00:18:38,203
Not because Pennsylvania
is losing its importance.

420
00:18:38,287 --> 00:18:39,163
It isn't.

421
00:18:39,247 --> 00:18:42,843
And not because Amish families are
abandoning Pennsylvania in one giant

422
00:18:42,927 --> 00:18:43,963
migration.

423
00:18:44,047 --> 00:18:46,107
The real answer is more interesting.

424
00:18:46,287 --> 00:18:50,103
Pennsylvania shows what happens
when Amish communities grow deep.

425
00:18:50,187 --> 00:18:53,187
Wisconsin shows what
happens when they grow wide.

426
00:18:53,367 --> 00:18:54,703
In Pennsylvania,

427
00:18:54,787 --> 00:18:58,103
generations have piled into some
of the oldest and largest Amish

428
00:18:58,187 --> 00:18:59,647
settlements on Earth.

429
00:18:59,787 --> 00:19:03,823
One geographic community can
contain hundreds of congregations.

430
00:19:03,907 --> 00:19:04,903
In Wisconsin,

431
00:19:04,987 --> 00:19:08,923
different waves of families have
repeatedly found new rural areas,

432
00:19:09,007 --> 00:19:10,623
formed separate communities,

433
00:19:10,707 --> 00:19:13,243
and created a much more fragmented map.

434
00:19:13,327 --> 00:19:15,243
Pennsylvania built giants.

435
00:19:15,327 --> 00:19:17,203
Wisconsin built dots.

436
00:19:17,287 --> 00:19:20,687
And the smallest dots may
be the ones worth watching

437
00:19:20,827 --> 00:19:23,943
because Lancaster was once
a small settlement too.

438
00:19:24,027 --> 00:19:26,443
Cashton was once a handful of families.

439
00:19:26,527 --> 00:19:28,367
Kingston was once new.

440
00:19:28,487 --> 00:19:33,303
Every giant Amish community began with
someone making the same basic decision.

441
00:19:33,387 --> 00:19:35,803
There is not enough
room for our future here.

442
00:19:35,887 --> 00:19:37,583
Let's try somewhere else.

443
00:19:37,667 --> 00:19:39,123
A farm is purchased.

444
00:19:39,207 --> 00:19:40,907
Another family follows.

445
00:19:41,047 --> 00:19:42,683
A congregation forms.

446
00:19:42,767 --> 00:19:44,083
Children are born.

447
00:19:44,167 --> 00:19:45,723
The district divides.

448
00:19:45,807 --> 00:19:47,943
And if enough people remain long enough,

449
00:19:48,027 --> 00:19:52,927
one tiny mark on a map becomes
a community that lasts for generations.

450
00:19:53,027 --> 00:19:56,942
That is the real meaning behind
Wisconsin's sixty-eight settlements.

451
00:19:57,026 --> 00:20:00,403
The Amish population is
not only getting larger,

452
00:20:00,487 --> 00:20:03,023
it is spreading into more
versions of itself.

453
00:20:03,107 --> 00:20:05,603
And that leaves an even
more interesting question.

454
00:20:05,687 --> 00:20:09,663
If a handful of Amish families find
cheap land somewhere new tomorrow,

455
00:20:09,747 --> 00:20:13,923
what exactly has to happen before they
can call it a real Amish settlement?

456
00:20:14,007 --> 00:20:16,444
Because buying the farms
is only the beginning

